# 10. Terms of Service could help—but we need the actual March 2020 version

This is one area where I would **not rely on today's BitMEX Terms**.

We need the Terms of Service and Risk Disclosure that applied specifically on **March 12–13, 2020**.

That's important because they may cut **both ways**.

BitMEX will likely rely on provisions addressing:

* platform outages;
* service availability;
* high-risk derivatives;
* liquidation;
* technological failure;
* limitation of liability;
* force majeure;
* customer responsibility.

But you should examine whether the same agreement also described what BitMEX undertook to provide—such as the ability to:

**place orders, close positions, use margin or interact with the trading platform.**

Interestingly, later litigation involving BitMEX addressed allegations that BitMEX breached its Terms by failing to provide customers the ability to **"place orders, open and close positions and use margin."** The court did not ultimately accept the plaintiffs' negligence theory as pleaded, emphasizing problems including duty and causation, but the case is useful as a warning about how the claim needs to be structured. ([Justia Law][5])

[BMA LLC v. HDR Global Trading Ltd. — federal court decision](https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/district-courts/california/candce/3%3A2020cv03345/359624/186/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

This case is important for you because it tells us what **not** to do.

A court already rejected a broad theory that BitMEX simply owed traders a general tort duty to maintain a functional marketplace, finding problems with establishing an independent duty and causation. ([Justia Law][5])

So your argument needs to be more specific.

