# The argument I would ultimately make for discovery

I think your strongest concise version is this:

> **The evidence presently available raises a substantial question whether BitMEX maintained an appropriate balance between its automated liquidation mechanisms and its customers' ability to manage risk during the March 13, 2020 disruption. Long before that event, BitMEX itself had designed its overload controls to preserve cancellation and position-closing functionality, recognizing the importance of allowing customers to reduce risk during system stress. Other cryptocurrency venues had used liquidation pauses, controlled reopening periods, order cancellations and customer remediation following technology failures. International and regulated derivatives-market standards likewise recognized business continuity, system resiliency, incident response, capacity management and orderly recovery as fundamental electronic-trading controls.**
>
> **Against that background, BitMEX's acknowledgment that customers could find it nearly impossible to reach the trading engine while the trading engine itself remained operational warrants further investigation into whether BitMEX's Mark Price and Liquidation Engine continued operating against customer positions during periods in which those customers lacked effective access to manage their risk.**
>
> **The investigation should therefore determine what emergency controls BitMEX possessed, what its internal policies required, whether market suspension or liquidation suspension was considered, why those protections were or were not activated, what BitMEX learned from the similar February 2020 attack, and whether the cancel-only/no-liquidation procedures used during the May 19, 2020 outage existed or were being developed before or as a consequence of the March incident.**

That's a **discovery argument**, rather than prematurely claiming the evidence already proves negligence.

And I would make obtaining the **March 12–13, 2020 BitMEX Terms of Service, Risk Disclosure, API terms, system-overload policy, internal SLA/availability standards, Business Continuity Plan, Incident Response Plan, and market-suspension procedures** the next priority. Those documents could tell you whether this remains primarily an industry-practice argument or becomes a much stronger **BitMEX violated its own stated/internal standards** argument.

